---
title: "Add live planning pool items"
pr: 96
module: acme_planning
---

{% section id="mental-model" navLabel="Why this PR exists" icon="git-compare" group="Orientation" title="Why this PR exists" %}

Reviewing this with a queue behind you? The diff is wider than the idea. Here's the idea.

When a quote is confirmed, the workload behind it becomes real but unscheduled. A line might read *Dana Lopez owes Project Atlas six days of site surveys in March 2027*: agreed and sold, sitting on zero calendars. Someone, usually the manager, still has to drop those days onto actual dates, and until this PR no screen told them how much of that confirmed work was still waiting for one.

{% callout tone="key" %}
**"Shared pool"** is the team's name for it: the pile of confirmed-but-unscheduled work. Open a confirmed project, click **Remaining to Schedule**, and you get one row per chunk of it: `Dana Lopez · Project Atlas · March 2027 · 6 d total · 4 placed · 2 left`. The planner works that list down to zero. (PRD: EF-CON-3, EF-PLN-3.)
{% /callout %}

### The one thing to check the rest of the diff against

The pool stores none of those numbers. "2 left" isn't a column that gets decremented when a shift appears; it's `max(total − placed, 0)`, recomputed the instant you read it, where `placed` is just the summed hours of every shift pointing back at that same confirmed allocation. So `planning.pool.item` is a SQL view, a saved query over the allocations, not a table of its own. Create a shift and the remaining drops. Resize it, cancel it, delete it, hand it to a different allocation, and the figure tracks it every time.

{% flow steps=["CRM opportunity", "allocation (forecast)", "quote confirmed (converted)", "pool row shows up", "planner places shifts", "2 left → 0"] /%}

### What it deliberately doesn't do

Nothing here generates shifts for you. That's a deliberate rule, not a missing feature: never bulk-create dated shifts without a human deciding (EF-CON-3). For now a planner sets **Source Allocation** on a shift by hand, and that one link is the whole engine. The generation wizard is later, in #62; when it ships it must push slots through this exact link instead of inventing a second way to drain the pool.

{% /section %}

{% section id="m-pool" navLabel="planning_pool_item.py" icon="database" group="Models" title="planning.pool.item" %}

A read-only **SQL view** model (`_auto = False`). Grain: **one row per source allocation**, so prestation, task and allocation identity survive into planning.

Model attributes: `_name = "planning.pool.item"`, `_auto = False`, `_rec_name = "name"`.

### Fields

{% fields %}
{% field name="name" type="Char · compute" %}
Human label: *project – service – role – month – person*.
{% /field %}
{% field name="allocation_id" type="Many2one → planning.allocation" %}
The source row this lens reflects. Readonly.
{% /field %}
{% field name="opportunity_id / demand_id" type="Many2one" %}
→ `crm.lead` / `planning.allocation.demand`, carried from the allocation.
{% /field %}
{% field name="product_id / role_id / employee_id" type="Many2one" %}
→ service / `planning.role` / `hr.employee` (empty = *To assign*).
{% /field %}
{% field name="person_or_role_name" type="Char · compute" %}
Named person, or "To assign". Reads `allocation.assignee_name`.
{% /field %}
{% field name="project_id / task_id" type="Many2one" %}
→ `project.project` / `project.task`, gained at conversion.
{% /field %}
{% field name="analytic_account_id" type="Many2one" %}
→ `account.analytic.account`.
{% /field %}
{% field name="month" type="Date" %}
Allocation month — the planning bucket.
{% /field %}
{% field name="quantity_unit" type="Selection" %}
`days` / `hours` — the unit the totals are expressed in.
{% /field %}
{% field name="total / total_hours" type="Float · compute" %}
**Charge to schedule** = allocation quantity (and in hours).
{% /field %}
{% field name="placed / placed_hours" type="Float · compute" %}
**Already planned** = Σ `allocated_hours` of non-cancelled linked slots.
{% /field %}
{% field name="remaining / remaining_hours" type="Float · compute" %}
**Reste à planifier** = `max(total − placed, 0)`.
{% /field %}
{% field name="company_id" type="Many2one → res.company" %}
Drives the record rules.
{% /field %}
{% /fields %}

### Methods

{% method sig="init(self)" %}
Drops any existing view, then `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW` selecting allocations `WHERE status = 'converted' AND active IS TRUE`. The allocation `id` becomes the pool item `id`.
{% /method %}

{% method sig="_compute_quantities(self)" decorator="@api.depends('allocation_id.quantity', 'allocation_id.quantity_hours', 'allocation_id.planning_slot_ids.allocated_hours', 'allocation_id.planning_slot_ids.state')" %}
Calls the allocation's shared aggregator `_planning_placed_hours_by_id()`, then fills total / placed / remaining in both unit and hours, flooring remaining at zero.
{% /method %}

The class declaration and the view definition — note `_auto = False` on the model, and the `WHERE` clause that keeps only active Converted allocations in the view.

{% diff file="addons/acme_planning/models/planning_pool_item.py" from=10 to=44 mark="12" /%}

The compute walks the linked slots through the shared aggregator, so the readings are derived at read time — never stored, never decremented.

{% diff file="addons/acme_planning/models/planning_pool_item.py" from=46 to=68 mark="52-58" /%}

{% callout tone="key" %}
**Why a SQL view and not a stored model?** A copied pool table would create a second charge store that issue #60 and #62 would have to reconcile. A view can't drift: it *is* the allocations.
{% /callout %}

{% /section %}
